\title{When an order of a plan stops working, and what is done with it then}
\description{
The lifetime of an order in a plan: when it stops, which part of its life that bounds, and what is done then.

A lifetime ends exactly one way: \code{at_time}, a time of day on the instrument's next trading day; \code{after_minutes}, counted from when the plan is placed; \code{after_days}, 1 to 365 days of 24 hours from then, which keeps the plan alive across trading days; or \code{when}, any trigger condition, checked on every tick. \code{applies_to} says whether the end bounds the wait for the trigger, the time the order works after it is sent, or both, which is UBI's default. An order still waiting when its end comes is done as expired. An order working then ends by \code{on_end}: \code{cancel}, UBI's default, cancels what rests and keeps what filled; \code{marketable} moves what rests two ticks past the other side's touch so it fills, which a stop cannot do; and \code{close_filled} cancels what rests and closes what filled at market, which only a plan that is this one order can do, and not an order that protects a position. A part ended by \code{close_filled} is done with the reason \code{closed}, or \code{expired} when nothing had filled.

Unlike the other parts, a lifetime is an entry of the order's \code{lifetime} list rather than a value under one key, so its \code{document()} holds the settings directly. A \code{RepeatPart} with \code{until} gives each copy a lifetime of its own, so an order repeated that way takes no \code{Lifetime}.
}
